menu "Deep sleep"

config SLEEP_DEEP_ENABLE
    bool "Deep sleep between PIR wakes and sync sessions"
    default n
    depends on READER_PIR_GPIO >= 0
    help
        Battery work 2026-11-03. With the bolt engaged, no feedback running,
        no sync session due and no motion for READER_PIR_ACTIVE_MS, the chip
        enters deep sleep. It wakes on a PIR going high (ext1, any of the
        configured PIRs) or on the RTC timer when the next sync session is
        due. A wake restarts the firmware: the bolt is NOT driven again
        (it stayed engaged), the allow-list and the event ring come from
        NVS, the clock base and the sync schedule from RTC memory.

        There is no fallback poll while asleep: a covered PIR means a card
        is not read until someone moves in front of it.

config SLEEP_BOOT_AWAKE_S
    int "Stay awake after power-on / reset (s): provisioning console window"
    default 120
    range 10 3600
    depends on SLEEP_DEEP_ENABLE
    help
        The UART console only answers while the chip is awake. After a
        power-on or a reset (EN button) it stays awake this long; console
        input keeps it awake for SLEEP_CONSOLE_IDLE_S more.

config SLEEP_CONSOLE_IDLE_S
    int "Console input keeps the chip awake this long (s)"
    default 60
    range 5 3600
    depends on SLEEP_DEEP_ENABLE

config SLEEP_PIR_STUCK_S
    int "A PIR high this long without a break is ignored (s)"
    default 60
    range 10 3600
    depends on SLEEP_DEEP_ENABLE
    help
        A stuck-high PIR would keep the chip awake forever (ext1 wakes on a
        high level). Past this time it stops counting as motion and is left
        out of the wake mask until it drops again.

endmenu
